BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Frenzy with Guillermo del Toro
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260628
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260629
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dda52-83b8-2223-cb75-2263d6b43651@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, June 28\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 56m\n\n1972 | 116 min. | UK | Color | English | Ra
 ted R | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Alfred Hitchcock \nWRITTEN BY: Anthony Shaffe
 r \nWITH: Jon Finch\, Alec McCowen\, Barry Foster\, Billie Whitelaw\n\nTyp
 e: Screenings\n\nDirector and lifelong Alfred Hitchcock fan Guillermo del 
 Toro delivers an in-depth lecture on this nail-biter from the Master of Su
 spense\, followed by a screening. \nA down-on-his-luck RAF veteran (Jon Fi
 nch) finds himself the prime suspect in series of rape-murders in the penu
 ltimate film from Hitchcock. Making his only film to receive an R rating a
 t the time of its release—and filming in his native England for the firs
 t time in more than two decades—Hitchcock took advantage of the new perm
 issiveness of 70s filmmaking with an unusually brutal and bleakly witty th
 riller\, working from a clever screenplay by Anthony Shaffer (Sleuth\, 197
 2) based on Arthur La Bern’s 1966 novel Goodbye Piccadilly\, Farewell Le
 icester Square\, complete with the master’s usual meticulous visuals and
  classic suspense set pieces.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/frenzy-with-guillermo
 -del-toro-019dda52-83b8-2223-cb75-2263d6b43651
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Let’s Make Love
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260628
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260629
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dda60-0589-9655-e49d-f0fa19d1ec52@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, June 28\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 59m\n\n1960 | 119 min | USA | Color | English | No
 t Rated | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: George Cukor  \nWRITTEN BY: Norman Krasna \
 nWITH: Marilyn Monroe\, Yves Montand\, Tony Randall\, Frankie Vaughan\n\nT
 ype: Screenings\n\nDirector George Cukor’s musical comedy was Monroe’s
  second-to-last completed film. She plays a bohemian off-Broadway actor wh
 o is in a show satirizing a French billionaire\, Jean-Marc Clément (Yves 
 Montand). While scoping out the production\, Clément is cast in the play 
 and the two fall in love\, though deception threatens to ruin everything. 
 Monroe worked closely with costume designer Dorothy Jeakins to draw inspir
 ation from her personal style for the film’s looks.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/lets-make-love-019dda
 60-0589-9655-e49d-f0fa19d1ec52
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:All That Money Can Buy (The Devil and Daniel Webster)
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260628
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260629
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019d8808-51f2-84ef-4692-307b79ea47f5@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, June 28\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 47m\n\n1941 | 107 min. | USA | Black-and-White | E
 nglish | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: William Dieterle \nWRITTEN BY: Dan Totheroh\, 
 Stephen Vincent Benét \nWITH: Edward Arnold\, Walter Huston\, Jane Darwel
 l\, Simone Simon\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA beleaguered farmer (James Craig)
  sells his soul to the devil only to enlist the help of famed attorney Dan
 iel Webster (frequent Capra villain Edward Arnold) to break the contract i
 n this stylish fantasy directed by Oscar nominee William Dieterle (The Lif
 e of Emile Zola\, 1937) and based on the short story by Stephen Vincent Be
 nét. Walter Huston was nominated for his lively performance as the Satani
 c Mr. Scratch\, and Bernard Herrmann won an Oscar for his second film scor
 e\; his first\, Citizen Kane\, was also nominated that same year. Herrmann
  incorporated classic folk songs such as “On Springfield Mountain” whi
 le using his typically inventive orchestrations to dazzling effect.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-devil-and-daniel-
 webster-019d8808-51f2-84ef-4692-307b79ea47f5
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Hitch-Hiker
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260629
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260630
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd575-e18f-fbe3-842e-c38d02a9579a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, June 29\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 11m\n\n1953 | 71 min. | USA | Black-and-White | En
 glish\, Spanish | Not Rated | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Ida Lupino \nWRITTEN BY: 
 Ida Lupino\, Collier Young \nWITH: Edmond O’Brien\, Frank Lovejoy\, Wil
 liam Talman\, José Torvay\n\nType: Screenings\n\nDirector Ida Lupino subm
 erges her viewer in the true story of serial killer Billy Cook\, who murde
 red six people over a three-week period in the early 1950s. In Lupino’s 
 fictionalized adaptation\, co-written by her career and life partner\, Col
 lier Young\, the threat of death looms over two friends who are kidnapped 
 by a psychotic wanderer during their fishing vacation. As the duo is force
 d to ferry the criminal across the US-Mexico border\, Lupino maintains a d
 ense air of tension that ratchets ever skyward during the picture’s swif
 t runtime. Considered by some to be the first noir helmed by a female film
 maker\, The Hitch-Hiker is also a standout within Lupino’s filmography\,
  fitting in right alongside other gritty thrillers of the era
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-hitch-hiker-019dd
 575-e18f-fbe3-842e-c38d02a9579a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Whisper of the Heart
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260629
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260630
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd4f7-e228-20c6-bae7-3b5767b0f59e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, June 29\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 51m\n\n1995 | 111 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese 
 with English subtitles | Rated G | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Yoshifumi Kondo \nWR
 ITTEN BY: Hayao Miyazaki. \nWITH: Yoko Honna\, Issei Takahashi\, Takashi 
 Tachibana\, Shigeru Muroi\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe first and only featur
 e directed by veteran Studio Ghibli animator Yoshifumi Kondo\, from a scri
 pt by Hayao Miyazaki\, Whisper of the Heart is a wistful coming-of-age sag
 a. One day\, bookworm Shizuku finds a cat riding the same train as her. Sh
 e follows it to an antique shop\, where a peculiar statuette of a dapper f
 eline named Baron Humbert von Gikkingen sends her imagination reeling. A t
 ale of crushes\, daydreams\, and friendships\, Whisper of the Heart bears 
 the stamp of Studio Ghibli’s superior craftmanship.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/whisper-of-the-heart-
 019dd4f7-e228-20c6-bae7-3b5767b0f59e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Galaxy Quest in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260701
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260702
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd529-253c-f148-74e3-19386a610226@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: director Dean Parisot and Academy Governor Debra Za
 ne\nSelected by the Casting Directors Branch\n\nWednesday\, July 1\, 2026\
 n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 43m\n\n1999 
 | 103 min. | USA | Color | Scope | English | Rated PG | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY:
  Dean Parisot \nWRITTEN BY: David Howard\, Robert Gordon \nSTORY BY: David
  Howard \nWITH: Tim Allen\, Sigourney Weaver\, Alan Rickman\, Tony Shalhou
 b\nPrint courtesy of the Dean Parisot Collection at the Academy Film Archi
 ve.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe stars of a long-canceled science-fiction TV
  series reluctantly reunite for a fan convention only to find themselves o
 n an unexpected adventure\, thanks to an endangered alien race who mistake
  them for genuine intergalactic heroes. With a witty script that pokes fun
  at actors\, science fiction\, and its fandom\, Galaxy Quest has become a 
 classic in the decades since its release. Director Dean Parisot and castin
 g director Debra Zane assembled an eclectic yet stellar cast\, including T
 im Allen as the self-important star\; Sigourney Weaver\, Alan Rickman\, To
 ny Shalhoub\, Sam Rockwell and Daryl Mitchell as his long-suffering co-sta
 rs\; Enrico Colantoni and Missi Pyle as friendly aliens\; and Justin Long 
 in his feature film debut as an eager fan.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/galaxy-quest-019dd529
 -253c-f148-74e3-19386a610226
END:VEVENT
