BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Some Like It Hot in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260702
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dda6b-59cc-be2f-090e-f2f741d4e082@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Pickle the Drag Queen\n\nThursday\, July 2\, 2026\n
 \nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 1m\n\n1959 | 
 121 min | USA | Black-and-White | English | Not Rated | 4K \nDIRECTED BY:
  Billy Wilder  \nWRITTEN BY: Billy Wilder\, I. A. L. Diamond \nWITH: Mar
 ilyn Monroe\, Tony Curtis\, Jack Lemmon\, George Raft\n\nType: Screenings\
 n\nMarilyn Monroe’s work in director Billy Wilder’s comedic classic is
  often cited as her best. The film was controversial for its racy humor an
 d story about two musicians (Tony Curtis and Jack Lemmon) who disguise the
 mselves as women to evade the Mafia. But of equal scandal were the Oscar-w
 inning costumes made by Orry-Kelly for Monroe’s Sugar Kane Kowalczyk. Bo
 th dresses are on display in the exhibit Marilyn Monroe: Hollywood Icon\, 
 on view through February 28\, 2027. Some Like It Hot was made during a yea
 r of dramatic highs and lows for Monroe\, with this film standing as a hig
 h point.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/some-like-it-hot-019d
 da6b-59cc-be2f-090e-f2f741d4e082
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Tokyo Drifter
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260702
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd578-9525-65a2-2a4f-39807145503e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, July 2\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 22m\n\n1966 | 82 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese 
 | Not Rated | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Seijun Suzuki.\nWRITTEN BY: Kohan Kawauchi
 \nWITH: Tetsuya Watari\, Chieko Matsubara\, Tamio Kawaji\, Ryuji Kita.\n\n
 Type: Screenings\n\nSeijun Suzuki takes a deep dive into a delirious world
  of yakuza\, centering around a killer named Tetsuya\, fresh out of prison
 \, who gets pulled into a gang war despite his wish to stay out of it. Suz
 uki’s visual orchestration is unhinged\, with its infinite flow of vivid
 \, stimulating colors clashing with the anticipated\, coherent style of th
 e narrative. It’s a liberated departure from the conventional structure 
 of storytelling—more similar to experimental film work. An unusual portr
 ayal of yakuza drama\, this thriller offers surreal\, sensational signifie
 rs through the exuberance of its highly stylized set design\, its bizarrel
 y choreographed blocking\, and offbeat pacing of its cuts.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/tokyo-drifter-019dd57
 8-9525-65a2-2a4f-39807145503e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Misfits in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260704
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dda6a-6f44-f26c-6d86-98e042029c2b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, July 3\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 5m\n\n1961 | 125 min | USA | Black-and-White | Engl
 ish | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: John Huston \nWRITTEN BY: Arthur
  Miller \nWITH: Clark Gable\, Marilyn Monroe\, Montgomery Clift\, Thelma 
 Ritter \nRestored by the Academy Film Archive and The Film Foundation. Res
 toration funding provided by the Hobson/Lucas Family Foundation.\n\nType: 
 Screenings\n\nPenned by Arthur Miller\, this contemporary Western centers 
 on the recently divorced Roslyn (Marilyn Monroe) and her relationship with
  an aging former cowboy\, Gay (Clark Gable)\, who now survives by rounding
  up wild mustangs to sell them to a slaughterhouse. Considered a commercia
 l failure at the time of its original release\, the film has since been re
 garded as a classic by critics and audiences\, perhaps notably because it 
 was the final completed film of both Gable and Monroe\, and a fitting book
 end to Monroe’s career—she credited Huston for her first big break in 
 his The Asphalt Jungle (1950).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-misfits-019dda6a-
 6f44-f26c-6d86-98e042029c2b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Sorcerer
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260704
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd579-9263-515c-5873-11100ff31dac@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, July 3\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 1m\n\n1977 | 121 min. | USA | Color | English/Spani
 sh/French/German | Rated R | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: William Friedkin\nWRITTEN B
 Y: Walon Green\nWITH: Roy Scheider\, Bruno Cremer\, Francisco Rabal\, Amid
 ou\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA thief (Roy Scheider) on the run after a heist 
 gone wrong find himself marooned in a South American village\, where his o
 nly chance to turn his life around is to join three equally desperate men 
 on a potentially suicidal mission: transporting two truckloads of nitrogly
 cerin over treacherous mountain roads. For his first film after the one-tw
 o punch of The French Connection (1971) and The Exorcist (1973)\, director
  William Friedkin remade Henri-Georges Clouzot’s classic 1953 thriller T
 he Wages of Fear (itself based on Georges Arnaud’s 1950 novel)\, staging
  breathtaking action scenes on remote locations. A box-office disappointme
 nt at the time of its release\, Sorcerer has since become one of Friedkin
 ’s most enduring classics\, bolstered by its Oscar-nominated sound mixin
 g (Robert Knudson\, Robert J. Glass\, Richard Tyler\, Jean-Louis Ducarme) 
 and Tangerine Dream’s influential electronic score.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/sorcerer-019dd579-926
 3-515c-5873-11100ff31dac
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Whisper of the Heart
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260703
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260704
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd4f9-7849-b1b0-965c-537bb69da545@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, July 3\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 51m\n\n1995 | 111 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese w
 ith English subtitles | Rated G | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Yoshifumi Kondo \nWRI
 TTEN BY: Hayao Miyazaki. \nWITH: Yoko Honna\, Issei Takahashi\, Takashi T
 achibana\, Shigeru Muroi\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe first and only feature
  directed by veteran Studio Ghibli animator Yoshifumi Kondo\, from a scrip
 t by Hayao Miyazaki\, Whisper of the Heart is a wistful coming-of-age saga
 . One day\, bookworm Shizuku finds a cat riding the same train as her. She
  follows it to an antique shop\, where a peculiar statuette of a dapper fe
 line named Baron Humbert von Gikkingen sends her imagination reeling. A ta
 le of crushes\, daydreams\, and friendships\, Whisper of the Heart bears t
 he stamp of Studio Ghibli’s superior craftmanship.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/whisper-of-the-heart-
 019dd4f9-7849-b1b0-965c-537bb69da545
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jaws in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260704
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019db107-26cd-12fd-87e7-a1ebc697e1fa@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, July 4\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 4m\n\n1975 | 124 min | USA | Color | Scope | Engl
 ish | Rated PG | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Steven Spielberg \nWRITTEN BY: Pete
 r Benchley\, Carl Gottlieb \nWITH: Roy Scheider\, Robert Shaw\, Richard D
 reyfuss\, Lorraine Gary\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSteven Spielberg’s second
  feature as a director set the standard for edge-of-your-seat suspense and
  quickly became a cultural phenomenon. When the seaside community of Amity
  Island falls prey to a great white shark\, the chief of police (Roy Schei
 der)\, a young marine biologist (Richard Dreyfuss)\, and a grizzled shark 
 hunter (Robert Shaw) embark on a desperate quest to destroy the beast befo
 re it strikes again. A box office record-breaker as well as a Best Picture
  nominee\, Jaws earned Oscars for film editing\, sound\, and John Williams
 ’s thrilling score.\nOn the occasion of Jaws: The Exhibition—the large
 st mounted exhibition ever organized around Spielberg’s film\, experienc
 e the movie before diving into the collection. The museum’s first of thi
 s scale focused exclusively on a single film\, the exhibition features sce
 ne breakdowns\, interactive experiences\, behind-the-scenes stories\, and 
 more than 200 original objects\, many never before seen on public display\
 , and follows the structure of the film\, taking visitors from the opening
  credits to its gripping conclusion. \nJaws: The Exhibition is curated by 
 Jenny He\, senior exhibitions curator\, and Emily Rauber Rodriguez\, assis
 tant curator\, with Alexandra James Salichs\, curatorial assistant\, Acade
 my Museum of Motion Pictures.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/jaws-019db107-26cd-12
 fd-87e7-a1ebc697e1fa
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Great Muppet Caper in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260704
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019db104-9ede-2724-6ea8-30871fa1a1f9@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, July 4\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 37m\n\n1981 | 97 min. | UK\, USA | Color | Englis
 h | Rated G | 4K\nDIRECTED BY: Jim Henson \nWRITTEN BY: Tom Patchett\, Ja
 y Tarses\, Jerry Juhl\, Jack Rose \nWITH: Jim Henson\, Frank Oz\, Dave Go
 elz\, Jerry Nelson\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThis second Muppets feature film
  casts Kermit the Frog\, Fozzie Bear\, and the Great Gonzo as intrepid jou
 rnalists on location in London to interview fashion icon Lady Holiday abou
 t the recent theft of her jewels. Hired as Holiday’s secretary is aspiri
 ng model Miss Piggy\, who poses as her boss when Kermit arrives. Mistaken 
 identities\, espionage\, and hijinks abound in this kooky escapade that fe
 atures cameos from comedy greats Charles Grodin\, John Cleese\, and intern
 ational raconteur Peter Ustinov. The film received an Academy Award nomina
 tion for Music (Original Song) for the delightful “The First Time It Hap
 pens.”
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-great-muppet-cape
 r-019db104-9ede-2724-6ea8-30871fa1a1f9
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Three Days of the Condor
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260706
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd57b-abd2-99cd-0867-db4e92aa0321@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 5\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 1m\n\n1977 | 121 min. | USA | Color | English/Spani
 sh/French/German | Rated R | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Sydney Pollack\nWRITTEN BY:
  Lorenzo Semple Jr.\, David Rayfiel \nWITH: Robert Redford\, Faye Dunaway\
 , Cliff Robertson\, Max von Sydow\n\nType: Screenings\n\nJoe Turner (Rober
 t Redford) is part of a team analyzing printed media for the CIA\, but whe
 n his seemingly harmless job turns unexpectedly deadly\, he finds himself 
 on the run from both his own government and forces unknown. The fourth of 
 seven collaborations between Robert Redford and director Sydney Pollack\, 
 this classic of 70s paranoid cinema\, based on James Grady’s novel Six D
 ays of the Condor\, benefits from a stellar cast (including Faye Dunaway a
 s Redford’s unwitting hostage and Max von Sydow as a surprisingly reason
 able assassin)\, Owen Roizman’s moody cinematography\, and the Oscar-nom
 inated editing of Fredric Steinkamp and Don Guidice.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/three-days-of-the-con
 dor-019dd57b-abd2-99cd-0867-db4e92aa0321
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Private Property
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260706
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd57a-e7c4-4cd9-514c-266e25cf7f85@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 5\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 19m\n\n1960 | 79 min. | USA | Black-and-White | Eng
 lish | Not Rated | 4K DCP.\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Leslie Stevens \nWITH: C
 orey Allen\, Warren Oates\, Kate Manx\, Robert Wark \nPrint courtesy of U
 CLA Film & Television Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA pair of drifters e
 merges from the Southern California ocean and hitches a ride to the well-a
 ppointed neighborhood of Beverly Hills\, where the twosome squats in an ab
 andoned home. Next door\, a sex-deprived housewife struggles to resist the
  duo’s mysterious magnetism\, prompting psychological turmoil\, guilt\, 
 and a host of quandaries about female agency. Made on a shoestring budget 
 independent from a major studio\, Leslie Stevens’s disquieting debut pro
 mpted the condemnation of the Catholic Legion of Decency\, while the MPAA 
 denied the film its stamp of approval.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/private-property-019d
 d57a-e7c4-4cd9-514c-266e25cf7f85
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Giant in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260705
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260706
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019d8807-8f35-89b4-4423-642b22f044e1@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 5\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 3h 18m\n\n1956 | 198 min. | USA | Color | English | Ra
 ted G | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: George Stevens \nWRITTEN BY: Fred Guiol\, Iv
 an Moffat \nWITH: Elizabeth Taylor\, Rock Hudson\, James Dean\, Carroll Ba
 ker\n\nType: Screenings\n\nGeorge Stevens (A Place in the Sun\, 1951) earn
 ed his second Directing Oscar for this epic yet character-centered adaptat
 ion of Edna Ferber’s 1952 bestseller about a wealthy Texan (Best Actor n
 ominee Rock Hudson)\, his blueblood bride (Elizabeth Taylor)\, and an ambi
 tious ranch hand (Best Actor nominee James Dean\, in his final performance
 ). The film received 10 nominations including Best Picture\, Supporting Ac
 tress (Mercedes McCambridge) and for Dimitri Tiomkin’s original score. F
 or his only collaboration with Stevens\, the Ukraine-born Tiomkin contribu
 ted authentic musical Americana\, including the memorable theme song “Th
 is Then Is Texas” (lyric by Paul Francis Webster).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/giant-019d8807-8f35-8
 9b4-4423-642b22f044e1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:30th Anniversary of Bottle Rocket with Wes Anderson
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260706
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260707
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019de523-ce0e-d4b4-b0d9-578590072521@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: filmmaker Wes Anderson\n\nMonday\, July 6\, 2026\n\
 nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 31m\n\n1996 | 
 91 min. | USA | Color | English\, Spanish | Rated R | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY:
  Wes Anderson \nWRITTEN BY: Owen C. Wilson\, Wes Anderson  \nPRODUCED BY
 : Cynthia Hargrave\, Polly Platt \nWITH: Luke Wilson\, Owen C. Wilson\, N
 ed Dowd\, Shea Fowler\n\nType: Screenings\n\nFor 30 years\, writer-directo
 r Wes Anderson has cultivated a meticulous visual style that conjures laye
 rs of nostalgia while charting a unique and deeply personal cinematic lang
 uage. With its sharp comedic beats and deadpan performances from brothers 
 Luke and Owen Wilson\, Anderson’s debut feature\, Bottle Rocket\, announ
 ces this first chapter of his burgeoning sensibility—and reminds us he
 ’s possessed his singular aesthetic all along. \nFilmed on location in a
 nd around Dallas\, this playful caper\, co-written with co-star Owen Wilso
 n and executive produced by James L. Brooks (Terms of Endearment\, 1983)\,
  blends the fleetfooted spirit of the French New Wave with the gritty\, re
 al-world stakes of early 1990s crime films for an indie gem that marks an 
 auspicious start for one of American cinema’s most consistently visionar
 y talents.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/bottle-rocket-30th-an
 niversary-019de523-ce0e-d4b4-b0d9-578590072521
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Night of the Hunter in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260706
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260707
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd57c-a7a5-928e-8172-063ab7ffc190@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, July 6\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 33m\n\n1955 | 93 min. | USA. | Black-and-White | En
 glish | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Charles Laughton\nWRITTEN BY: James Agee\nWITH:
  Robert Mitchum\, Shelley Winters\, Lillian Gish\, James Gleason\n\nType: 
 Screenings\n\nPulitzer Prize winner James Agee adapted the 1953 novel by D
 avis Grubb for this stylish and evocative suspense drama\, which was the o
 nly feature directed by Oscar -winning actor Charles Laughton (The Private
  Life of Henry VIII\, 1933). Robert Mitchum is Harry Powell\, a remorseles
 s murderer who travels the countryside in the guise of a preacher.  He set
 s his sights on the widow (Shelley Winters) and children of a former cellm
 ate\, who may hold the key to a hidden fortune. Working in black-and-white
  with cinematographer Stanley Cortez (The Magnificent Ambersons\, 1942)\, 
 Laughton creates a dream-like world of danger for the children\, anchored 
 by the performance of screen legend Lillian Gish as their potential savior
 .
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/night-of-the-hunter-0
 19dd57c-a7a5-928e-8172-063ab7ffc190
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Princess Mononoke in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260706
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260707
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd4fa-4622-28a9-6f79-b9f62112f72c@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, July 6\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 13m\n\n1997 | 133 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese w
 ith English subtitles | Rated PG-13 | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Hayao Miy
 azaki \nWITH: Yoji Matsuda\, Yuriko Ishida\, Yuko Tanaka\, Kaoru Kobayash
 i\nNew subtitled print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive\, with special
  thanks to Studio Ghibli.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHayao Miyazaki’s intern
 ational breakthrough is an epic cinematic marvel. Infected by a deadly cur
 se after killing a god\, young warrior Ashitaka heads west in search of a 
 cure. He stumbles upon a bitter conflict between Lady Eboshi\, the proud p
 eople of Iron Town\, and the enigmatic Princess Mononoke\, a girl raised b
 y wolves and who will stop at nothing to prevent humans from destroying he
 r home\, and the forest spirits and animal gods who live there. Princess M
 ononoke resists an easy dichotomy of good versus evil and was the first Mi
 yazaki film that incorporated computer-generated imagery alongside traditi
 onal hand-drawn animation.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/princess-mononoke-019
 dd4fa-4622-28a9-6f79-b9f62112f72c
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Lovers on the Bridge (Les amants de Pont-Neuf) in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260708
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260709
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd52e-d672-d769-b172-eb2edced096f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Selected by the Cinematographers Branch\n\nWednesday\, July 8\
 , 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 5m\n\
 n1991 | 125 min. | France | Color | French | Rated R | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED/W
 RITTEN BY: Leos Carax \nWITH: Juliette Binoche\, Denis Lavant\, Klaus-Mich
 ael Grüber\, Marion Stalens\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAn artist losing her e
 yesight (Oscar-winner Juliette Binoche) and an unhoused circus performer (
 Denis Lavant) form an unexpected connection on the streets of Paris in thi
 s visually extravagant romance from writer-director Leos Carax (Holy Motor
 s\, 2012). Carax worked on a modest scale on his first two films\, Boy Mee
 ts Girl (1984) and Mauvais Sang (1986)\, but The Lovers on the Bridge was 
 an elaborate and infamously troubled project\, plagued by production delay
 s\, budget overruns\, and the necessity to recreate Paris’s Pont-Neuf on
  a massive set. The result received mixed reactions upon its release but h
 as since been celebrated as one of the filmmaker’s most towering achieve
 ments\, enhanced greatly by the dazzling cinematography of Jean-Yves Escof
 fier (Good Will Hunting\, 1997).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-lovers-on-the-bri
 dge-019dd52e-d672-d769-b172-eb2edced096f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Princess Mononoke in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260708
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260709
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd4fb-ae21-2e16-f346-ad206ac21b04@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, July 8\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 13m\n\n1997 | 133 min. | Japan | Color | Japanes
 e with English subtitles | Rated PG-13 | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Hayao 
 Miyazaki \nWITH: Yoji Matsuda\, Yuriko Ishida\, Yuko Tanaka\, Kaoru Kobay
 ashi\nNew subtitled print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive\, with spec
 ial thanks to Studio Ghibli.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHayao Miyazaki’s int
 ernational breakthrough is an epic cinematic marvel. Infected by a deadly 
 curse after killing a god\, young warrior Ashitaka heads west in search of
  a cure. He stumbles upon a bitter conflict between Lady Eboshi\, the prou
 d people of Iron Town\, and the enigmatic Princess Mononoke\, a girl raise
 d by wolves and who will stop at nothing to prevent humans from destroying
  her home\, and the forest spirits and animal gods who live there. Princes
 s Mononoke resists an easy dichotomy of good versus evil and was the first
  Miyazaki film that incorporated computer-generated imagery alongside trad
 itional hand-drawn animation.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/princess-mononoke-019
 dd4fb-ae21-2e16-f346-ad206ac21b04
END:VEVENT
