BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:How Does the Silver Screen Shape the Golden State? Featuring Frida
 y
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260710
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260711
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019e8942-5046-8528-2ad4-4cdd6167d315@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations,Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, July 10\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 37m\n\n1995 | 97 min. | USA | Color | English | Ra
 ted R | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: F. Gary Gray \nWRITTEN BY: Ice Cube\, DJ Pooh
  \nWITH: Ice Cube\, Chris Tucker\, Nia Long\, John Witherspoon \nSchedule
 \n7–7:40pm | Pre-screening conversation | Ted Mann Theater\n7:40–9pm |
  Friday screening | Ted Mann Theater\n9:15–9:45pm | Post-screening mixer
  | TMT Lobby\n\nType: Conversations\, Screenings\n\nThis screening of Frid
 ay (1995) and conversation is co-presented by the Academy Museum and Zóca
 lo Public Square. It's part of Zócalo’s California 175 — What Connect
 s California? series\, a suite of free Zócalo events and journalism bring
 ing together leaders and thinkers from all walks of life to envision Calif
 ornia’s next 175 years. \nCinema has always held a special place for the
  California imagination\, often recreating its cities\, beaches\, and wild
 erness through a lens of riches\, romance\, and reinvention—the Californ
 ia Dream. \nBut does that dream still exist? As Hollywood faces new challe
 nges from streaming\, AI\, and industry flight\, is the California imagina
 tion changing? How about the way the world sees the Golden State? Can it r
 emain the epicenter of cinematic storytelling through the rest of the 21st
  century? \nAbout the filmIt’s Friday\, and recently unemployed Craig (I
 ce Cube) has no job. With nothing to do\, he links up with his stoner frie
 nd Smokey (Chris Tucker) in their South Central Los Angeles neighborhood. 
 What starts as a simple day spirals into a raucous adventure as the two se
 ek to settle a debt with drug dealer Big Worm (Faizon Love)\; navigate ecc
 entric locals\; and dodge local bully Deebo (Tiny “Zeus” Lister Jr.). 
 \nCo-written by Ice Cube and filmed on location\, Friday paints a loving t
 ribute to the South LA area where he grew up\, drawing on personal experie
 nces to craft a light-hearted tale filled with memorable characters\, quot
 able lines\, and stories of Southland life that have resonated worldwide. 
 \nAbout our co-hostsFounded in Los Angeles in 2003\, Zócalo Public Square
  is a unit of ASU Media Enterprise and syndicates journalism to media outl
 ets worldwide. It has hosted more than 750 free events in cities including
  New York\, Washington D.C.\, Chicago\, Houston\, San Francisco\, Shanghai
 \, Mexico City\, London\, and Berlin. To learn more\, visit zocalopublicsq
 uare.org\, or like and follow on Instagram\, Facebook\, X\, YouTube\, and 
 LinkedIn.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/how-does-the-silver-s
 creen-shape-the-golden-state-featuring-friday-1995-019e8942-5046-8528-2ad4
 -4cdd6167d315
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Blow Out in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260710
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260711
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd57d-7000-1a17-a7ea-f705d9ff2f9c@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, July 10\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 48m\n\n1981 | 108 min. | USA | Color | Scope | Eng
 lish | Rated R | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Brian De Palma \nWITH: John T
 ravolta\, Nancy Allen\, John Lithgow\, Dennis Franz\n\nType: Screenings\n\
 nBrian De Palma wrote and directed this mystery about a movie sound techni
 cian (John Travolta) who inadvertently records the assassination of a poli
 tician. Stylish and unexpectedly emotional\, Blow Out has become one of De
  Palma’s most acclaimed films over the last four decades\, and his scrip
 t cleverly incorporates filmmaking techniques into its storyline. In colla
 boration with sound mixer Dick Vorisek and sound editor Dan Sable\, as wel
 l as such De Palma regulars as cinematographer Vilmos Zsigmond\, film edit
 or Paul Hirsch\, and composer Pino Donaggio\, the director creates a worth
 y successor to the great conspiracy thrillers of the 1970s.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/blow-out-019dd57d-700
 0-1a17-a7ea-f705d9ff2f9c
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Careful in 4K with Guy Maddin
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260711
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd537-2e34-6c78-8d7f-584841b78a06@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:US restoration premiere\nIn person: director Guy Maddin and fi
 lmmaker and author Sandi Tan\n\nSaturday\, July 11\, 2026\n\nLocation: Aca
 demy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 40m\n\n1992 | 100 min. | Can
 ada | Color | English | Not Rated | 4K DCP  \nDIRECTED BY: Guy Maddin  \
 nWRITTEN BY: Guy Maddin\, George Toles \nSTORY BY: George Toles \nWITH: 
 Kyle McCulloch\, Gosia Dobrowolska\, Sarah Neville\, Brent Neale\n\nType: 
 Screenings\n\nGuy Maddin’s early masterpiece takes place in a 19th-centu
 ry Alpine village where the wary residents—adult\, child and animal!—m
 ust speak softly and tread lightly lest they cause an avalanche. But sexua
 l frenzies teem in this world of repression\, setting off incestuous love 
 triangles and quadrangles with deadly consequences. Bathed in lurid\, lumi
 nescent tints\, Careful resembles a vintage melodrama from another planet
 —something that could only emerge from the singular mind of Maddin. —Z
 eitgeist Films
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/careful-with-guy-madd
 in-019dd537-2e34-6c78-8d7f-584841b78a06
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Elevator to the Gallows
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260711
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd57e-11da-98b1-87d7-2de5abc187cd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, July 11\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 33m\n\n1958 | 93 min. | France | Black-and-White
  | French | Not Rated | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Louis Malle\nWRITTEN BY: Louis M
 alle\, Roger Nimier \nWITH: Jeanne Moreau\, Maurice Ronet\, Georges Poujou
 ly\, Yori Bertin\n\nType: Screenings\n\nLouis Malle’s directorial debut 
 at age 24 is one of the most significant contributions to the French New W
 ave. The sensational and eloquently mesmerizing Jeanne Moreau stars as Flo
 rence\, whose hopeless adoration of a secret lover\, Julien (Maurice Ronet
 )\, jeopardizes her freedom. The couple plans a high-stakes murder of Flor
 ence’s husband\, and Julien successfully executes the plot—until he ge
 ts trapped in an elevator. A series of ironic incidents ensue against the 
 backdrop of a modernized landscape of Paris\, while the cinematography is 
 beautifully paired with a magical musical performance by the great Miles D
 avis. It all comes together to amplify Florence’s deep emotional state i
 n the deep night of a desolate\, apathetic city.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/elevator-to-the-gallo
 ws-019dd57e-11da-98b1-87d7-2de5abc187cd
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Clue
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260711
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019db101-caa9-1616-9f8c-b6a9c029251c@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, July 11\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 28m\n\n1985 | 88 min. | USA | Color | English | 
 Rated PG | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Jonathan Lynn \nWRITTEN BY: Jonathan Lynn
  \nWITH: Eileen Brennan\, Tim Curry\, Madeline Kahn\, Christopher Lloyd\n
 \nType: Screenings\n\nThis darkly comedic adaptation of the popular Parker
  Brothers board game takes a page from the spate of Agatha Christie book-t
 o-screen projects of the 1970s. Convening a murderer’s row of ’80s com
 edic actors\, this cult-favorite whodunit may have been considered middlin
 g upon its release but soon found its adoring audience on home video and t
 hrough revival screenings. This DCP from Paramount features all three endi
 ngs shown sequentially\, as they were presented during television broadcas
 ts of the film and as featured on home video releases.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/clue-019db101-caa9-16
 16-9f8c-b6a9c029251c
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:My Winnipeg in 35mm with Guy Maddin
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd539-00fa-31a2-67b6-c57af87fcdb1@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 12\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 20m\n\n2007 | 80 min. | Canada | Black-and-White\,
  Color | English | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Guy Maddin \nWRITTEN BY
 : Guy Maddin\, George Toles \nWITH: Ann Savage\, Louis Negin\, Amy Stewar
 t\, Darcy Fehr\n\nType: Screenings\n\nCommissioned as a documentary about 
 the director’s prairie hometown\, My Winnipeg proceeds from the simple p
 remise that a city is nothing without its ghosts: the mass of memories\, l
 egends and fictions that accumulate in any place inhabited by human beings
 . My Winnipeg is a film about the dream life of a city\, a para-documentar
 y about a parallel\, phantasmagorical\, invisible Winnipeg\; it is a séan
 ce\, a channeling of spirits through the medium of cinema\, an exorcism of
  personal and collective history\, as well as a memoir of one soul’s des
 perate final attempt at an escape from the city and its ghosts. —Tyler P
 rozeniuk\, POV Magazine
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/my-winnipeg-with-guy-
 maddin-019dd539-00fa-31a2-67b6-c57af87fcdb1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Green Fog with Guy Maddin
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd538-3ef3-53b8-2fc6-aaf84e3fc5dd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 12\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 3m\n\n2017 | 63 min. | USA | Color | English | Not
  Rated | DCP  \nDIRECTED BY: Guy Maddin\, Evan Johnson\, Galen Johnson \
 nWITH: Rock Hudson\, Karl Malden\, Chuck Norris\, John Saxon (archive foot
 age)\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe Green Fog is a new version\, in a wonderfu
 lly oblique fashion\, of Hitchcock’s Vertigo\; it could also have been n
 amed San Francisco Plays Itself or\, to borrow a title from Peter Greenawa
 y\, Vertical Features Remake. Hitchcock’s film is one of the most vertic
 ally obsessed films ever\, and The Green Fog follows suit\, but since this
  is a work co-directed by Guy Maddin\, you can imagine that it’s also ob
 sessive on many other levels that Hitchcock could never have dreamed of. T
 he Green Fog tells a story of sorts\, essentially reproducing the shape of
  Vertigo—with some room for facetiously digressive play—using clips fr
 om assorted films and TV series\, all shot on location in San Francisco\, 
 to stand in for moments of Hitchcock’s film. —Jonathan Romney\, Film C
 omment
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-green-fog-with-gu
 y-maddin-019dd538-3ef3-53b8-2fc6-aaf84e3fc5dd
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Magnificent Seven in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019d8807-479d-3bdf-e11e-5f3a97e1777d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 12\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 8m\n\n1960 | 128 min. | USA | Color | Scope | Engl
 ish\, Spanish | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: John Sturges \nWRITTEN BY: William R
 oberts \nWITH: Yul Brynner\, Eli Wallach\, Steve McQueen\, Horst Buchholz\
 n\nType: Screenings\n\nYul Brynner plays a gunslinger leading a septet of 
 strangers to defend a Mexican village against marauders in director John S
 turges’ classic Western remake of Akira Kurosawa’s Seven Samurai—a r
 emake that itself inspired three sequels\, a TV series\, and its own 2016 
 remake. Brynner’s fellow gunslingers include then-rising stars Steve McQ
 ueen\, Charles Bronson and James Coburn. Elmer Bernstein received his seco
 nd Oscar nomination for his rousing score\, whose classic main theme becam
 e arguably his most popular and widely recognized melody.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-magnificent-seven
 -019d8807-479d-3bdf-e11e-5f3a97e1777d
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Saddest Music in the World with Guy Maddin
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260714
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd53b-afab-95c2-4ae6-6e0f0c70c508@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, July 13\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 40m\n\n2003 | 100 min. | Canada | Color\, Black-an
 d-White | English\, Spanish | Rated R | DCP  \nDIRECTED BY: Guy Maddin. 
  \nWRITTEN BY: Kazuo Ishiguro\, George Toles\, Guy Maddin.  \nWITH: Mark
  McKinney\, Isabella Rossellini\, Maria de Medeiros\, David Fox\n\nType: S
 creenings\n\nGuy Maddin’s The Saddest Music in the World exists in a tim
 e and place we have never seen before\, although it claims to be set in Wi
 nnipeg in 1933. The city\, we learn\, has been chosen by the London Times\
 , for the fourth year in a row\, as “the world capital of sorrow.” Her
 e Lady Port-Huntley (Isabella Rossellini) has summoned entries for a conte
 st which will award $25\,000 “in Depression Era dollars” to the perfor
 mer of the saddest music. The Canadian filmmaker has devised a style that 
 evokes old films from an alternate timeline\; The Saddest Music is not sil
 ent and not entirely in black-and-white\, but it looks like a long-lost cl
 assic from decades ago\, grainy and sometimes faded\; he shoots on 8mm fil
 m and video and blows it up to look like a memory from cinema’s distant 
 past…You have never seen a film like this before\, unless you have seen 
 other films by Guy Maddin.  —Roger Ebert\, Chicago Sun-Times
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-saddest-music-in-
 the-world-with-guy-maddin-019dd53b-afab-95c2-4ae6-6e0f0c70c508
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:My Neighbors the Yamadas
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260714
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd511-a205-b46b-3a95-3db863a98edd@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, July 13\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 44m\n\n1999 | 104 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese 
 with English subtitles | Rated PG | DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Isao Takahat
 a \nWITH: Yukiji Asaoka\, Toru Masuoka\, Masako Araki\, Hayato Isohata\n\
 nType: Screenings\n\nIsao Takahata’s adaptation of Hisaichi Ishii’s be
 loved slice-of-life manga departs from Studio Ghibli’s traditional anime
  style and instead uses a roughly sketched comic-strip look. Awash in past
 el watercolors\, My Neighbors the Yamadas delights in the everyday misadve
 ntures of its titular family—dad\, mom\, older brother\, little sister\,
  granny\, and Pochi the dog. Punctuated by daydreams and fantasies\, Takah
 ata’s film is a heart-warming and honest look at modern family life. Alo
 ng with its typical animation style\, Ghibli forgoes its usual long\, orch
 estral music in favor of short piano themes to match its episodic style\, 
 making the film a standout in the studio’s filmography.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/my-neighbors-the-yama
 tas-019dd511-a205-b46b-3a95-3db863a98edd
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bugsy in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd52f-a256-6605-687a-101422312e89@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: producer Mark Johnson and production designer Denni
 s Gassner\nSelected by the Costume Designers Branch\n\nWednesday\, July 15
 \, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 15m\
 n\n1991 | 135 min. | USA | Color | English | Rated R | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY:
  Barry Levinson \nWRITTEN BY: James Toback \nWITH: Warren Beatty\, Annett
 e Bening\, Harvey Keitel\, Joe Mantegna \nPrint courtesy of the Academy Fi
 lm Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nOscar winner Barry Levinson (Rain Man\,
  1988) directed this lavish biographical gangster drama about the notoriou
 s Benjamin “Bugsy” Siegel (Best Actor nominee Warren Beatty)\, his rom
 ance with the beautiful Virginia Hill (Annette Bening)\, and his role in t
 he transformation of Las Vegas into a gambling mecca. The film earned 10 O
 scar nominations\, including Best Picture\, Directing\, Original Screenpla
 y\, Cinematography\, and the supporting performances of Harvey Keitel (as 
 Mickey Cohen) and Ben Kingsley (as Meyer Lansky). Oscars were awarded for 
 Art Direction (Dennis Gassner\, Nancy Haigh) and the elegant\, expressive\
 , and period-perfect costume designs of two-time winner Albert Wolsky (All
  That Jazz\, 1979)
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/bugsy-019dd52f-a256-6
 605-687a-101422312e89
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:My Neighbors the Yamadas
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd512-359a-f02d-e72c-7e577b814208@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, July 15\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 44m\n\n1999 | 104 min. | Japan | Color | Japane
 se with English subtitles | Rated PG | DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Isao Taka
 hata \nWITH: Yukiji Asaoka\, Toru Masuoka\, Masako Araki\, Hayato Isohata
 \n\nType: Screenings\n\nIsao Takahata’s adaptation of Hisaichi Ishii’s
  beloved slice-of-life manga departs from Studio Ghibli’s traditional an
 ime style and instead uses a roughly sketched comic-strip look. Awash in p
 astel watercolors\, My Neighbors the Yamadas delights in the everyday misa
 dventures of its titular family—dad\, mom\, older brother\, little siste
 r\, granny\, and Pochi the dog. Punctuated by daydreams and fantasies\, Ta
 kahata’s film is a heart-warming and honest look at modern family life. 
 Along with its typical animation style\, Ghibli forgoes its usual long\, o
 rchestral music in favor of short piano themes to match its episodic style
 \, making the film a standout in the studio’s filmography.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/my-neighbors-the-yama
 tas-019dd512-359a-f02d-e72c-7e577b814208
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Juice with Ernest Dickerson
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260716
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260717
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd57f-69a3-8bdd-8d5a-ac677c12b91a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, July 16\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1992 | 95 min. | USA | Color | English | 
 Rated R | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Ernest R. Dickerson \nWRITTEN BY: Ernest R.
  Dickerson\, Gerard Brown \nWITH: Omar Epps\, Tupac Shakur\, Jermaine Hop
 kins\, Khalil Kain\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe directorial debut of Ernest 
 R. Dickerson\, who previously lensed Spike Lee’s Do The Right Thing (198
 9) and Malcolm X (1992)\, trains its sights on four Black teenagers in Har
 lem. While Q (Omar Epps) is obsessed with becoming a DJ and the “juice
 ” it would bring to his reputation\, ringleader Raheem (Khalil Kain) has
  bigger scores in mind. When a seemingly low-stakes bodega robbery goes aw
 ry\, bullied Bishop (Tupac Shakur) allows the allure of power and violence
  get to his head. Dickerson’s confident crime film is steeped in neoreal
 ism and noir traditions and fuels its narrative with ‘90s hip-hop—Too 
 $hort\, Salt-N-Pepa\, Fabulous Five Inc.\, Cypress Hill—buoyed by an ele
 ctric performance from Tupac Shakur.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/juice-with-ernest-dic
 kerson-019dd57f-69a3-8bdd-8d5a-ac677c12b91a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Poison Ivy in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260717
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260718
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd57f-e1cb-bdce-e97c-4a394e83a8eb@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: cinematographer Phedon Papamichael and director Kat
 t Shea\n\nFriday\, July 17\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pi
 ctures\n\nRuntime: 1h 33m\n\n1992 | 93 min. | USA | Color | English | Rate
 d R | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Katt Shea Ruben \nWRITTEN BY: Andy Ruben\, Katt 
 Shea Ruben \nWITH: Tom Skerritt\, Sara Gilbert\, Cheryl Ladd\, Drew Barrym
 ore \nPrint courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nW
 ith her dad experiencing a mid-life crisis and mom sick and bed-ridden\, S
 ylvie Cooper (Sara Gilbert) has resorted to lying and bomb threats to give
  her life meaning\, until she befriends the mysterious and seductive Ivy (
 Drew Barrymore). Ivy easily infiltrates this dysfunctional family\, and as
  her deceit and manipulation creates chaos and tragedy\, she shatters this
  family to make a spot for herself. Filled with classic tropes of the erot
 ic thriller including a femme fatale\, illicit seduction\, and murder\, Po
 ison Ivy is often described as a teenage Fatal Attraction. It went on to s
 pawn three direct-to-home-video sequels.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/poison-ivy-019dd57f-e
 1cb-bdce-e97c-4a394e83a8eb
END:VEVENT
