BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Wallace & Gromit: The Curse of the Were-Rabbit
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260725
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260726
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dda3a-cfae-2718-68de-f7bf62b2c9bf@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Accessible screening\n\nSaturday\, July 25\, 2026\n\nLocation:
  Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 25m\n\n2005 | 85 minutes
  | UK | Color | English | Open Captions | DCP  \nDIRECTED BY: Nick Park\,
  Steve Box.  \nWRITTEN BY: Steve Box\, Nick Park\, Mark Burton\, Bob Bake
 r.  \nWITH: Peter Sallis\, Ralph Fiennes\, Helena Bonham Carter\, Peter K
 ay\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAccessible screeningOn the last Saturday of ever
 y month\, the museum's Family Matinee is an accommodative screening where 
 we offer open captioning\, keep the theater dimly lit\, and maintain a low
 er volume specifically for neurodivergent viewers. \nAardman Animations’
  beloved stop-motion characters make their feature-length debut in this ir
 reverent creature feature\, in which the cheese-loving Wallace and his fai
 thful dog\, Gromit\, contend with a giant were-rabbit pillaging the vegeta
 bles of their village. The first stop-motion film to have won the Animated
  Feature Oscar\, its voice cast includes such notables as Helena Bonham Ca
 rter and Ralph Fiennes.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/wallace-gromit-the-cu
 rse-of-the-were-rabbit-019dda3a-cfae-2718-68de-f7bf62b2c9bf
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cutter's Way
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260726
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260727
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd583-e1a7-e213-7c39-6dcfa44c2bce@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 26\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 49m\n\n1981 | 109 min. | USA | Color | English | R
 ated R | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Ivan Passer \nWRITTEN BY: Jeffrey Alan Fiskin 
 \nWITH: Jeff Bridges\, John Heard\, Lisa Eichhorn\, Ann Dusenberry\n\nType
 : Screenings\n\nIvan Passer (Intimate Lighting\, 1966) directed this low-k
 ey mystery\, based on Newton Thornberg’s novel Cutter and Bone. John Hea
 rd is Cutter\, a disabled\, hard-drinking Vietnam veteran\, and Jeff Bridg
 es is Bone\, his easygoing best friend. When Cutter becomes convinced that
  Bone is the witness to a young woman’s murder\, the resulting obsession
  challenges their friendship and threatens their lives. With its Santa Bar
 bara setting\, evocative cinematography by Jordan Cronenweth (Blade Runner
 \, 1982)\, and a dreamy score by Jack Nitzsche (One Flew Over the Cuckoo
 ’s Nest\, 1975)\, Cutter’s Way is a one-of-a-kind cross between a 70s-
 style character study and a paranoid thriller.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/cutters-way-019dd583-
 e1a7-e213-7c39-6dcfa44c2bce
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Blackmail
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260726
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260727
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd582-e5d1-4e52-31b8-2f41fd8359af@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 26\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 22m\n\n1929 | 82 min. | UK | Black-and-White | Eng
 lish | Not Rated\nDIRECTED BY: Alfred Hitchcock.\nWRITTEN BY: Alfred Hitch
 cock\, Benn W. Levy (dialogue)  \nWITH: Anny Ondra\, Sara Allgood\, Charl
 es Paton\, John Longden\n\nType: Screenings\n\nConsidered by many historia
 ns to be the first feature-length talkie out of Britain\, this seminal thr
 iller from the Master of Suspense cemented Alfred Hitchcock “as the most
  admired British director of all time” (Mark Duguid\, BFI Screenonline).
  Shot on location in the London Underground\, at the Tea House at Piccadil
 ly Circus\, and atop the British Museum\, Blackmail succeeds not just in i
 ts compelling mystery\, but also in capturing the richness of life in 1920
 s London. A picture that could have been a simple police procedural transc
 ends its roots in the theater to become an exemplar of cinematic formalism
  and a high watermark of Hitchcock’s early years\, with well-timed sonic
  emphases that could unnerve even the steeliest of dispositions.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/blackmail-019dd582-e5
 d1-4e52-31b8-2f41fd8359af
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Murder on the Orient Express
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260726
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260727
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019d8806-f1fa-e43f-575f-e24053f914be@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, July 26\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 8m\n\n1974 | 128 min. | UK | Color | English | Rat
 ed PG | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Sidney Lumet \nWRITTEN BY: Paul Dehn \nWITH: Al
 bert Finney\, Lauren Bacall\, Martin Balsam\, Ingrid Bergman\n\nType: Scre
 enings\n\nThe legendary detective Hercule Poirot (an unrecognizable Albert
  Finney\, in an Oscar-nominated performance) finds himself faced with a tr
 ain full of all-star suspects (including Sean Connery\, Anthony Perkins an
 d Vanessa Redgrave) when a notorious gangster (Richard Widmark) is murdere
 d on the Orient Express. Director Sidney Lumet took an unexpected departur
 e from his usual gritty stories of contemporary urban life with this lavis
 h film version of Agatha Christie’s 1934 whodunit. Ingrid Bergman won he
 r third Oscar for her droll supporting performance as an unassuming suspec
 t\, and the film’s six nominations included one for composer Richard Rod
 ney Bennett\; the three-time nominee contributed an elegant score highligh
 ted by a charming waltz theme for the train itself.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/murder-on-the-orient-
 express-019d8806-f1fa-e43f-575f-e24053f914be
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Painting Clouds with Studio Ghibli Artist Yoichi Nishikawa
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260729
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260730
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:e5c13dad-1a87-9719-cd81-526b8ea28c07@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, July 29\, 2026 at 6:30–8:30pm\n\nLocation: Shirl
 ey Temple Education Studio\, LL1\n\nCost: Free with advance reservation\n\
 nType: Conversations\n\nWatch a live painting demonstration by a Studio Gh
 ibli artist and see how the makers of the Ponyo (2008)\, The Boy and the H
 eron (2023)\, and other beloved films create such beautifully crafted scen
 ery. Studio Ghibli background artist and Yoichi Nishikawa\, known as “th
 e painter of clouds\,” speaks with Academy Museum Senior Exhibitions Cur
 ator Jessica Niebel before offering a rare\, in-person look at his waterco
 lor technique.
LOCATION:Shirley Temple Education Studio\, LL1
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/painting-clouds-with-
 yoichi-nishikawa-e5c13dad-1a87-9719-cd81-526b8ea28c07
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Who Framed Roger Rabbit
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260801
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260802
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019db102-6a44-5086-0897-939caa28feba@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, August 1\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 47m\n\n1988. 107 min. USA. Color. English. Rate
 d PG. DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Robert Zemeckis  \nWRITTEN BY: Jeffrey Price\, 
 Peter S. Seaman \nWITH: Bob Hoskins\, Christopher Lloyd\, Joanna Cassidy\
 , Charles Fleischer\n\nType: Screenings\n\nEddie Valiant (Bob Hoskins)\, a
  private eye in 1930s Los Angeles\, gets an unexpected client—Roger Rabb
 it\, a cartoon film star. Director Robert Zemeckis’s witty comedy-myster
 y is a dazzling mix of live-action and animation\, incorporating beloved c
 artoon characters from Disney and other studios. Nominated for its art dir
 ection\, cinematography\, and sound\, it won for its film editing\, sound 
 effects editing\, and visual effects. Richard Williams also received a Spe
 cial Achievement Award from the Academy for his animation direction.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/who-framed-roger-rabb
 it-019db102-6a44-5086-0897-939caa28feba
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Out of Africa in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260802
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260803
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019d8806-52fd-216b-45c9-4904d7d5b323@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, August 2\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 42m\n\n1985 | 162 min. | USA | Color | English | 
 Rated PG | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Sydney Pollack \nWRITTEN BY: Kurt Leudtke \
 nWITH: Robert Redford\, Meryl Streep\, Klaus Maria Brandauer\, Michael Kit
 chen\nPrint courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nT
 he memoir of Danish author Karen Blixen (published under her pen name Isak
  Dinesen) was adapted by director Sydney Pollack and screenwriter Kurt Leu
 dtke for this lavish romantic drama\, focusing on the years Blixen (Best A
 ctress nominee Meryl Streep) spent running a coffee farm in Nairobi and he
 r relationship with hunter Denys Finch-Hatton (Robert Redford). Out of Afr
 ica was nominated for 11 Oscars and won seven including Best Picture\, Dir
 ecting (both for Pollack)\, Adapted Screenplay\, Art Direction\, Cinematog
 raphy and Sound\, with John Barry winning his fourth Oscar for an achingly
  romantic score.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/out-of-africa-019d880
 6-52fd-216b-45c9-4904d7d5b323
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:A Man Escaped in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260803
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260804
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd584-92b4-fd24-11c8-7d0ce23af494@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, August 3\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 41m\n\n1956 | 101 min. | France | Black-and-White
  | French\, German | Not Rated | 35mm  \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Robert Bres
 son \nWITH: François Leterrier\, Charles Le Clainche\, Maurice Beerblock
 \, Roland Monod\n\nType: Screenings\n\n“On one side\, thick walls\, bars
 \, soldiers\, weapons. On the other\, one lone man\, unarmed\, doomed from
  the start.”\nIn minimalist Robert Bresson’s 1956 film\, a French Resi
 stance fighter attempts escape from a German military prison against impos
 sible odds. While the title of the film seems to say it all\, it belies th
 e film’s gripping tension and near-silent sequences of harrowing enginee
 ring feats\, unbelievable patience\, and human resilience\, all performed 
 with placid determination by François Leterrier as he embodies lieutenant
  Fontaine\, a proxy for André Devigny\, whose life Bresson loosely adapte
 d for the screen from his 1956 memoir.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/a-man-escaped-019dd58
 4-92b4-fd24-11c8-7d0ce23af494
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Cat Returns
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260803
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260804
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd517-16d7-e8e7-5dcb-d39a9c5fd257@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, August 3\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 15m\n\n2002 | 75 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese 
 with English subtitles | Rated G | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Hiroyuki Morita \nWR
 ITTEN BY: Reiko Yoshida \nWITH: Chizuru Ikewaki\, Yoshihiko Hakamada\, Ak
 i Maeda\, Takayuki Yamada\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA spin-off of Whisper of 
 the Heart\, The Cat Returns finds another schoolgirl encountering a myster
 ious tomcat on the street\, though this time she is led to a fantastic wor
 ld beyond her wildest dreams. Launched into a cobblestoned magic kingdom r
 uled by cat royalty—including a motorcade replete with vassals\, maidens
 \, and even Secret Service—she must enlist the help of the elusive Baron
  Humbert von Gikkingen (the same cat featured as a statue in Whisper of th
 e Heart) if she hopes to make it back home. The only feature film directed
  by Hiroyuki Morita to date\, The Cat Returns is a feline flight of fancy.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-cat-returns-019dd
 517-16d7-e8e7-5dcb-d39a9c5fd257
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Titicut Follies in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260805
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260806
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd531-3419-7f41-baa2-1ec05eb4f02f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: film editor and former Academy Governor Kate Amend 
 \nSelected by the Documentary Branch\n\nWednesday\, August 5\, 2026\n\nLoc
 ation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 27m\n\n1967 | 87 m
 in. | USA | Black-and-White | English | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Frederick Wis
 eman\n\nType: Screenings\n\nFilmmaker Frederick Wiseman spent 29 days in 1
 966 at the Bridgewater State Hospital in Massachusetts\, filming the treat
 ment and mistreatment of its patients. After a year of editing\, the resul
 t\, the first feature from the noted documentarian\, established his disti
 nctively observational filmmaking style. Banned from public exhibition for
  decades and finally approved for release on First Amendment grounds in a 
 1991 court decision\, Titicut Follies was selected for preservation by the
  National Film Registry in 2022. In 2016\, Wiseman—whose other classic d
 ocumentaries include High School (1968)\, Primate (1974) and Welfare (1975
 )— received an honorary Oscar for his “masterful and distinctive docum
 entaries [that] examine the familiar and reveal the unexpected.”
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/titicut-falls-019dd53
 1-3419-7f41-baa2-1ec05eb4f02f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
