DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260805
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260806
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd517-e48c-ba35-4370-f1b96ed34a7a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, August 5\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Mot
 ion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 15m\n\n2002 | 75 min. | Japan | Color | Japane
 se with English subtitles | Rated G | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Hiroyuki Morita \
 nWRITTEN BY: Reiko Yoshida \nWITH: Chizuru Ikewaki\, Yoshihiko Hakamada\,
  Aki Maeda\, Takayuki Yamada\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA spin-off of Whisper 
 of the Heart\, The Cat Returns finds another schoolgirl encountering a mys
 terious tomcat on the street\, though this time she is led to a fantastic 
 world beyond her wildest dreams. Launched into a cobblestoned magic kingdo
 m ruled by cat royalty—including a motorcade replete with vassals\, maid
 ens\, and even Secret Service—she must enlist the help of the elusive Ba
 ron Humbert von Gikkingen (the same cat featured as a statue in Whisper of
  the Heart) if she hopes to make it back home. The only feature film direc
 ted by Hiroyuki Morita to date\, The Cat Returns is a feline flight of fan
 cy.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-cat-returns-019dd
 517-e48c-ba35-4370-f1b96ed34a7a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:In the Cut in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260806
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260807
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd585-40b8-73e6-0781-56750aa09dd3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, August 6\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 59m\n\n2003 | 119 min. | USA | Color | English 
 | Rated R | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Jane Campion \nWRITTEN BY: Jane Campion\, 
 Susanna Moore \nWITH: Meg Ryan\, Mark Ruffalo\, Jennifer Jason Leigh\, Ke
 vin Bacon\n\nType: Screenings\n\nJane Campion’s gritty erotic thriller s
 tars Meg Ryan as Frannie Avery\, a seemingly apathetic writer and English 
 teacher who finds solace in her daydream of language and literature. At a 
 bar near her apartment\, Frannie accidentally witnesses a sexual encounter
  between a woman and a man whose face she cannot recall. The woman is late
 r found dismembered\, spurring a visit from a detective who quickly become
 s Frannie’s infatuation. Paralleling the desolate ambience of the urban 
 landscape is Frannie’s facade\, until her suppressed emotions trigger ou
 tbursts that may lead her straight into the path of a serial killer of wom
 en.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/in-the-cut-019dd585-4
 0b8-73e6-0781-56750aa09dd3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Basic Instinct in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260807
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd587-0e22-9625-5806-8b921623f8a2@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, August 7\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 7m\n\n1992 | 127 min. | USA | Color | Scope | Eng
 lish | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Paul Verhoeven \nWRITTEN BY: Joe
  Eszterhas \nWITH: Michael Douglas\, Sharon Stone\, George Dzundza\, Jean
 ne Tripplehorn\n\nType: Screenings\n\nHardboiled San Francisco detective N
 ick Curran (Michael Douglas) is assigned to the case of a murdered rock st
 ar and gets in over his head when he meets the victim’s lover\, Catherin
 e Tramell (Sharon Stone). A crime novelist whose latest work mirrors Curra
 n’s case\, and past relationships with violent people\, Catherine become
 s the prime suspect. Confronted by Tramell’s smoldering sensuality and m
 ysterious past\, Curran struggles to maintain his professionalism and not 
 give into his carnal instincts. \nPaul Verhoeven’s erotic thriller made 
 a huge impact\, serving as a pop-culture reference for many years and defi
 ning the genre for the 1990s. The recipient of Academy Award nominations f
 or editing (Frank J. Urioste) and Jerry Goldsmith’s original score\, Bas
 ic Instinct\, originally edited for sexual and violent content\, has been 
 restored in 4K and is now available in Verhoeven’s original\, uncut vers
 ion.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/basic-instinct-019dd5
 87-0e22-9625-5806-8b921623f8a2
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Cat Returns
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260807
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd519-9fe3-64e5-f810-1c587e19cb45@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, August 7\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 15m\n\n2002 | 75 min. | Japan | Color | Japanese 
 with English subtitles | Rated G | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Hiroyuki Morita \nWR
 ITTEN BY: Reiko Yoshida \nWITH: Chizuru Ikewaki\, Yoshihiko Hakamada\, Ak
 i Maeda\, Takayuki Yamada\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA spin-off of Whisper of 
 the Heart\, The Cat Returns finds another schoolgirl encountering a myster
 ious tomcat on the street\, though this time she is led to a fantastic wor
 ld beyond her wildest dreams. Launched into a cobblestoned magic kingdom r
 uled by cat royalty—including a motorcade replete with vassals\, maidens
 \, and even Secret Service—she must enlist the help of the elusive Baron
  Humbert von Gikkingen (the same cat featured as a statue in Whisper of th
 e Heart) if she hopes to make it back home. The only feature film directed
  by Hiroyuki Morita to date\, The Cat Returns is a feline flight of fancy.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-cat-returns-019dd
 519-9fe3-64e5-f810-1c587e19cb45
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Popcorn Disabilities Conversation and Book Signing with Kristen Lo
 pez
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019ddf76-e982-c361-1f01-60e1b84b6d8b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Book Signings,Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Journalist\, author\, and disability advocate Kristen Lopez si
 gns copies of her book\, Popcorn Disabilities: The Highs and Lows of Disab
 led Representation in the Movies.\n\nSaturday\, August 8\, 2026\n\nLocatio
 n: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nType: Book Signings\, Conversation
 s\n\nJoin journalist\, author and disability advocate Kristen Lopez as she
  speaks with particular authority on how disability is represented—and t
 oo often misrepresented—in movies. It’s all examined in her book\, Pop
 corn Disabilities: The Highs and Lows of Disabled Representation in the Mo
 vies. \nPopcorn Disabilities is Lopez’s impassioned\, yet fun and engagi
 ng survey of how Hollywood has dealt with disability over the last century
 . She reveals that\, even when they're not just narrative props to help an
  able-bodied protagonist\, movie characters with disabilities are overwhel
 mingly white\, affluent\, and conventionally attractive\, obscuring the va
 riety of disabilities and the experiences of those who deal with them. But
  she also explains where films have gotten it right\, and how the power of
  the medium can enlighten and educate in the future. \nFrom little-remembe
 red gems such as Tod Browning's Freaks (1932)—one of the earliest attemp
 ts to show dimensionality in characters with disabilities—to contemporar
 y films such as CODA (2021)\, My Left Foot (1989)\, A Different Man (2024)
 \, and many others\, it challenges popular assumptions about disability wh
 ile never losing sight of movies' unique power\, influence\, and potential
  as a tool for social good.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/popcorn-disabilities-
 book-signing-with-kristen-lopez-019ddf76-e982-c361-1f01-60e1b84b6d8b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The 4th Man in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd589-d8d0-bf49-4cbf-adea87d13136@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, August 8\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 42m\n\n1983 | 102 min. | Netherlands | Color | 
 Dutch with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Paul Verhoev
 en\nWRITTEN BY: Gerard Soeteman\nWITH: Jeroen Krabbé\, Renée Soutendijk\
 , Thom Hoffman\, Dolf de Vries\nPrint courtesy of the ConstellationCenter 
 Collection at the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nIn Paul Ver
 hoeven’s stylish\, psychological-erotic thriller\, author Gerard (Jeroen
  Krabbé)\, begins a romantic affair with Christine (Renée Soutendijk)\, 
 an enigmatic widow from literary society\, only to become obsessed with he
 r lover\, Herman (Thom Hoffman). While continuing the affair in hopes of g
 etting closer to Herman\, Gerard is plagued by ominous visions\, fueling p
 aranoid thoughts about Christine plotting murder. Based on the novel by ce
 lebrated Dutch author Gerard Reve\, The 4th Man was Verhoeven’s final fi
 lm shot in Dutch before directing English-language and Hollywood productio
 ns. It was the Netherlands’ official submission for International Featur
 e Film at the 56th Academy Awards\, and\, upon its release in the US\, gav
 e American audiences a taste of Verhoeven’s unabashed take on sex\, viol
 ence\, and religion.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-4th-man-019dd589-
 d8d0-bf49-4cbf-adea87d13136
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fritz Lang’s M
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd589-21a2-cf22-47aa-909c46d0348a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Academy Governor Howard A. Rodman\n\nSaturday\, Aug
 ust 8\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h
  51m\n\n1931 | 111 min. | Germany | Black and white | German | PG-13 | DCP
 \nDIRECTED BY: Fritz Lang \nWRITTEN BY: Fritz Lang\, Thea von Harbou \nW
 ITH: Peter Lorre\, Otto Wernickle\, Inge Landgut\, Gustaf Gründgens\n\nTy
 pe: Screenings\n\nOne of the most influential filmmakers—whose innovativ
 e cinematic language led to such groundbreaking films such as Metropolis (
 1927) and Woman in the Moon (1929) —is Austrian-born Fritz Lang\, who co
 ntributed to the premise of sci-fi and noir film domains in American cinem
 a. Lang’s first sound film\, M\, is set in a 1930s Berlin disrupted by a
  gruesome serial murder case targeting children. Peter Lorre stars as the 
 creepy murderer\, Hans Beckert\, who embodies the instability and confusin
 g socio-political shifts that the nation was facing in the early 1930s. Pu
 rsued by the city police and criminals from the underworld\, Beckert’s h
 eightened anxiety is manifested through an array of daring close-up shots 
 in high-contrast\, stark\, black-and-white cinematography\, an apparent he
 ir of Lang’s early work in German Expressionism.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/fritz-langs-m-019dd58
 9-21a2-cf22-47aa-909c46d0348a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Harriet the Spy in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019db102-fe01-43a3-84a5-eeeb5fb60e21@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, August 8\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 40m\n\n1996 | 100 min. | USA | Color | English 
 | Rated PG | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Bronwen Hughes \nWRITTEN BY: Douglas Pe
 trie\, Theresa Rebeck. \nWITH: Michelle Trachtenberg\, Rosie O’Donnell\,
  Vanessa Lee Chester\, Gregory Smith\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe first moti
 on picture produced by Nickelodeon is also Bronwen Hughes’s feature film
  directorial debut\, starring Michelle Trachtenberg as a 12-year-old Harri
 et Welsch\, who devotes her time spying on everyone and recording her disc
 reet detective work in a “PRIVATE” notebook. When those secrets get ex
 posed\, Harriet finds her entire class against her\, including her two bes
 t friends. This sweet drama boldly and candidly surveys the bittersweet pr
 e-teen years as an exploration of self\, complementing the confusion that 
 marks this transitional phase with Harriet’s unapologetic\, cheeky spiri
 t\, letting her fight her own battles and attain redemption.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/harriet-the-spy-019db
 102-fe01-43a3-84a5-eeeb5fb60e21
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Elle
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd58b-36cb-2931-b14f-63ac39d62b9b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, August 9\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 10m\n\n2016 | 130 min. | France/Germany | Color |
  Scope | French | Rated R | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Paul Verhoeven\nWRITTEN BY: 
 David Birke \nWITH: Isabelle Huppert\, Laurent Lafitte\, Anne Consigny\, 
 Charles Berling\n\nType: Screenings\n\nMichèle (Isabelle Huppert)\, the h
 ead of a popular video game company\, is attacked in her home by a masked 
 assailant. Unaware of her attacker’s identity and suspicious of the men 
 in her life\, she begins to track down the criminal herself\, stepping int
 o a dangerous game of cat and mouse. Based on the award-winning novel Oh..
 . by Philippe Djian and propelled by Huppert’s Oscar-nominated performan
 ce\, Paul Verhoeven’s first French-language film was France’s official
  submission for International Feature Film for the 89th Academy Awards.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/elle-019dd58b-36cb-29
 31-b14f-63ac39d62b9b
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Thin Red Line in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019d8806-49c1-0b1c-0a34-0cd360c2e8e9@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, August 9\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 45m\n\n1998 | 165 min. | USA | Color | Scope | En
 glish | Rated R | 35mm \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Terrence Malick \nWITH: Sean
  Penn\, Adrien Brody\, Jim Caviezel\, Ben Chaplin\nPrint courtesy of the A
 cademy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nTerrence Malick adapted James 
 Jones’s 1962 novel\, inspired by the author’s experiences serving in W
 orld War II’s Guadalcanal campaign\, for his first film since his 1978 c
 lassic Days of Heaven. Malick assembled an all-star cast (including George
  Clooney\, Nick Nolte and John Travolta) for his unusually contemplative w
 ar epic\, which received seven Oscar nominations including Directing\, Ada
 pted Screenplay (both for Malick) and Best Picture. Nominated composer Han
 s Zimmer spent nine months working on his evocative score\, which he terme
 d the most challenging assignment of his career.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-thin-red-line-019
 d8806-49c1-0b1c-0a34-0cd360c2e8e9
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bunny Lake Is Missing in 4K
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd58a-8b76-50a9-ab86-a9842016a5b1@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, August 9\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion
  Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 47m\n\n1965 | 107 min. | USA | Black-and-White | 
 English | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Otto Preminger. \nWRITTEN BY:
  John Mortimer\, Penelope Mortimer \nWITH: Laurence Olivier\, Carol Lynle
 y\, Keir Dullea\, Noël Coward.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nOne of the most imp
 ortant auteurs of the 20th century cinema\, Otto Preminger is known for hi
 s bold exploration of themes challenging the societal norms of his era. Ca
 rol Lynley\, in an exceptional performance\, portrays Ann Lake\, a mother 
 recently moved to London from New York with her 4-year-old daughter\, Bunn
 y. When Bunny vanishes without a trace\, as if she never existed in the fi
 rst place\, Superintendent Newhouse excavates disturbing truths behind a f
 amily’s refined façade. Preminger finesses a subtle construction of atm
 ospheric tension\, amplified with a crisp\, black-and-white cinematography
  that articulates Ann’s psychological isolation and creates an eerie\, c
 laustrophobic ambience.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/bunny-lake-is-missing
 -019dd58a-8b76-50a9-ab86-a9842016a5b1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Prestige in 35mm
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd58c-400e-aae7-d42b-8937636a5c73@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, August 10\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 10m\n\n2006 | 130 min. | USA | Color | Scope | E
 nglish | Rated PG-13 | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Christopher Nolan\nWRITTEN BY: J
 onathan Nolan\, Christopher Nolan\nWITH: Hugh Jackman\, Christian Bale\, M
 ichael Caine\, Scarlett Johansson\nPrint courtesy of the Academy Film Arch
 ive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nTwo illusionists compete at the turn of the 20
 th century to create history’s greatest magic trick in this time-fractur
 ing thriller from director/co-writer Christopher Nolan. Based on the 1995 
 novel by Christopher Priest\, the film earned Oscar nominations for its ci
 nematography (Wally Pfister) and art direction (Nathan Crowley\, Julie Och
 ipinti)\, which cleverly used surviving Los Angeles picture palaces as key
  locations. The stellar cast includes Hugh Jackman and Christian Bale as t
 he rivals\, Michael Caine as their mentor\, Scarlett Johansson and Rebecca
  Hall as the women in their lives\, and a memorable cameo from David Bowie
  as the inventor Nikola Tesla.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-prestige-019dd58c
 -400e-aae7-d42b-8937636a5c73
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Howl’s Moving Castle
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd51d-7564-f197-c616-12ecc8530357@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, August 10\, 2026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motio
 n Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 59m\n\n2004 | 119 min. | Japan | Color | Japanes
 e with English subtitles | Rated PG | DCP\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Hayao Miya
 zaki \nWITH: Chieko Baisho\, Takuya Kimura\, Akihiro Miwa\, Tatsuya Gash
 ûin\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAn adaptation of Diana Wynne Jones’s fantasy
  novel of the same name\, the Oscar-nominated Howl’s Moving Castle is a 
 pitch-perfect fairy tale about aging and war\, set in a dazzling steampunk
  world. When young hatmaker Sophie is transformed into a 90-year-old woman
  by the vengeful Witch of the Waste\, she seeks to break the curse with th
 e help of the enigmatic wizard Howl\, the fire demon Calcifer\, and the ma
 gical moving castle they inhabit.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/howls-moving-castle-0
 19dd51d-7564-f197-c616-12ecc8530357
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:To Be or Not to Be
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
DTSTAMP:20260614T091037Z
UID:019dd531-8cec-e732-df70-044dfc38219b@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Selected by the Executives Branch\n\nWednesday\, August 12\, 2
 026\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 40m\n\n1
 942 | 100 min. | USA | Black-and-White | English | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Ernst
  Lubitsch \nWRITTEN BY: Edwin Justus Mayer \nSTORY BY: Melchior Lengyel \n
 WITH: Carole Lombard\, Jack Benny\, Robert Stack\, Felix Bressart\n\nType:
  Screenings\n\nA Shakespearean actor reluctantly joins a Resistance plot a
 gainst the Nazis in 1939 Warsaw in this daring comedy directed by two-time
  Oscar nominee Ernst Lubitsch (Heaven Can Wait\, 1943). Comedy legend Jack
  Benny had his greatest screen role as the pompous ham Joseph Tura\, and O
 scar nominee Carole Lombard (My Man Godfrey\, 1936) was his expert match a
 s his actress wife\, Maria\, her final film role before her death weeks be
 fore the film’s release. Finding the perfect balance between wartime ten
 sion and hilarious farce\, Lubitsch’s classic— filmed right as the U.S
 . entered World War II— was remade in 1983\, with Mel Brooks and Anne Ba
 ncroft as the couple.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/to-be-or-not-to-be-01
 9dd531-8cec-e732-df70-044dfc38219b
END:VEVENT
